% A form is never served a drawing another form was filed under.
%
% execform holds a form's drawing against a serial stamped on the form
% dictionary. The counter is the interpreter's own (.newformserial) and
% only ever moves forward, so no number is handed out twice.
%
% It cannot be a value in a dictionary. The number is stamped into the
% form dictionary, which is the program's and may be in either bank,
% while a counter would be in one of them: a restore past the execform
% that raised it winds the counter back, a dictionary the restore does
% not reach carries the old number forward, and the next form is issued
% the number that dictionary is still holding. The two then name one
% entry and whichever was captured first is painted for both. Nothing
% raises; the page carries the wrong shape. tests/font_serial_test.ps
% drives the same arrangement for the glyph masks.
%
% The cache itself stays in graphicsdict and is wound back by a restore.
% That costs a re-capture and never a wrong drawing, because the serial
% an entry was filed under is not issued again.
%
% Counting is the whole of it. A form raising no error says nothing about
% a cache: one that is never consulted raises none either. So every case
% counts how many times a description was run, and reads that count both
% ways -- a form that is held runs fewer times than it is placed, and one
% that is not held runs every time.
%
% Run through the ppm device by run-ppm-test.sh, which is a device a
% drawing can be held for. On a device that paints nothing every case
% takes the uncached path and none of this is reached.

/failures 0 def
/check { exch { pop }{ (FAIL: ) print print (\n) print
                       /failures failures 1 add def } ifelse } bind def

/PLACEMENTS 4 def

% Serials and run counts have to outlive the restores that are the point
% of this file, so they are kept in global memory: a restore takes back
% what was defined inside the save, and a number defined in there is
% exactly what has to be compared with the one issued afterwards.
currentglobal true setglobal
/seen 8 dict def
setglobal

% Place a freshly built form PLACEMENTS times and answer how many times
% its description was run. Each case builds its own form dictionary and
% its own counter, so no case can be answered by what an earlier one
% left behind. The gsave bracket is inside the loop, so a placement that
% raises still leaves the graphics stack as it was found.
/placed {  % (greylevel) . runs
    /pl.g exch def
    /pl.runs 0 def
    /pl.f << /FormType 1 /BBox [0 0 20 20] /Matrix matrix
             /PaintProc { pop /pl.runs pl.runs 1 add store
                          pl.g setgray 0 0 20 20 rectfill } >> def
    0 1 PLACEMENTS 1 sub {
        pop gsave 50.5 200.5 translate pl.f execform grestore
    } for
    seen /lastid pl.f /Implementation get put
    pl.runs
} bind def

% --- the negative control ---
% Before anything is asked about a restore, this file has to show that a
% form is held here at all. A run where nothing is ever cached would
% answer every case below with a description run at every placement,
% which is also what the defect looks like from the other side.
0 placed /r0 exch def
r0 PLACEMENTS lt (a form placed repeatedly is held) check
r0 0 gt (and its description is run at least once) check

% --- the counter is not wound back ---
% A form cached inside a save, the save restored, and another form after
% it. The premise is that the second form is issued a number the first
% one never had: the counter is the interpreter's, so the restore does
% not reach it.
/sv save def
0 placed pop
seen /idin seen /lastid get put
sv restore

1 placed /r2 exch def
seen /idafter seen /lastid get put

seen /idafter get seen /idin get gt
    (a form past a restore takes a serial the restore did not wind back) check

% Having been issued a number of its own, this form is run for itself
% rather than served what the earlier one was filed under.
r2 0 gt
    (a form past a restore is not served the earlier drawing) check
r2 PLACEMENTS lt
    (and is itself held once it has been run) check

% --- a form dictionary the restore does not reach ---
% The stamp goes into the program's own dictionary, which is the one part
% of the arrangement whose bank the program chooses. A form dictionary in
% global virtual memory keeps its number across a restore; what stops it
% naming a later form's drawing is that no later form is issued that
% number. Such a dictionary is held like any other -- the bank it lives
% in does not decide whether it can be.
currentglobal true setglobal
/gtally 4 dict def
gtally /runs 0 put
/GF << /FormType 1 /BBox [0 0 20 20] /Matrix matrix
       /PaintProc { pop gtally /runs gtally /runs get 1 add put
                    0 0 20 20 rectfill } >> def
setglobal

GF gcheck (the form dictionary this case needs is in global memory) check

% Placed repeatedly it is held, exactly as a local one is.
0 1 PLACEMENTS 1 sub {
    pop gsave 300.5 200.5 translate GF execform grestore
} for
gtally /runs get PLACEMENTS lt
    (a form dictionary in global memory is held) check
gtally /runs get 0 gt
    (and its description is run at least once) check
GF /Implementation known
    (and it takes a serial like any other) check

% And now the case the arrangement exists for, on a dictionary of its
% own so that nothing this file has already done can answer it. The form
% is placed for the first time inside a save, so the entry it is filed
% under is one the restore takes back -- while the dictionary carrying
% the number is not, because it is global.
%
% What must then be true is that no form issued afterwards is given that
% number. Both halves are read: the serial the next form takes, and
% whether the global form runs again for itself. A form served another
% form's drawing runs no second time and raises nothing, so the count
% alone would not tell the two apart -- which is why the serials are
% compared as well.
currentglobal true setglobal
/htally 4 dict def
htally /runs 0 put
/GH << /FormType 1 /BBox [0 0 20 20] /Matrix matrix
       /PaintProc { pop htally /runs htally /runs get 1 add put
                    0 0 20 20 rectfill } >> def
setglobal

/sv2 save def
gsave 450.5 200.5 translate GH execform grestore
sv2 restore

htally /runs get 1 eq (a global form placed inside a save paints there) check
/gid GH /Implementation get def

% a form issued a serial after the restore
0 placed pop
seen /lastid get gid ne
    (no form past the restore is issued the serial the global one carries) check

% and the global form, whose entry the restore took back, paints again
% for itself rather than being served what that form was filed under
gsave 450.5 200.5 translate GH execform grestore
htally /runs get 2 eq
    (a global form re-captures rather than taking another form's drawing) check

failures 0 eq
    { (SUCCESS\n) print }
    { failures =print ( failures\n) print } ifelse
flush
quit
